EXT. WHALEBONE TOLL BRIDGE - DUSK

A bridge made from the ribs of a dead sea-beast arches over a grey gorge. A tin lantern hangs at the midpoint.

BRANNOCH, fifties, broad, in a patched toll-keeper's coat, sits on a stool beside a rope barrier. He counts copper coins into a bowl.

A STRANGER in a travel-stained cloak walks up from the east road. Hood up. A staff in one hand.

BRANNOCH
Toll is two coppers. Four if you've a cart.

STRANGER
No cart.

Brannoch holds out a hand. The Stranger drops two coins in it. Brannoch bites one.

BRANNOCH
Good. Name for the ledger?

STRANGER
Do I need one?

BRANNOCH
Ledger likes a name. Keeps the dead from being counted twice.

The Stranger thinks. Pushes the hood back. A young woman, weathered, a scar through one eyebrow.

STRANGER
Tavi.

Brannoch writes it in a thick book.

BRANNOCH
Tavi. Where to?

TAVI
Over. Then the salt towns.

ILSEWYN (O.S.)
(from below the bridge)
Brannoch! The rope's slipped again!

Brannoch sighs and leans over the rail.

BRANNOCH
Hold it, then! I'm busy!

ILSEWYN (O.S.)
I am holding it! It's holding me!

BRANNOCH
(to Tavi)
My sister. She keeps the ferry cables.

TAVI
She sounds tired.

ILSEWYN (O.S.)
I am! And I can hear you!

BRANNOCH
She's been tired since our mother died.

Brannoch lifts the rope barrier. Tavi steps toward the span, then stops.

TAVI
Is it true the ribs sing at night?

BRANNOCH
When the wind comes from the north.

ILSEWYN (O.S.)
It does not sing, Brannoch, it whistles.

BRANNOCH
It sings, and always has.

ILSEWYN (O.S.) ^
It whistles, and always has.

Tavi looks from one to the other, almost smiling.

TAVI
Then I'll listen on the way across.

She walks onto the bridge. Brannoch watches her go, then sits and takes up the coins again.

BRANNOCH
Mind the third rib. It's loose.
